==============================================================================
[2] the degenerate channel: does the advance rate give the wind speed?
==============================================================================
    Four ways of measuring how far the fire got, because they fail
    differently and picking one in advance would be a choice, not a result:

             advance measure       n  r(log, log)      r^2
      distance to front, p90    1044        0.096   0.0093
      distance to front, p75    1044        0.083   0.0068
    distance to front, median    1044        0.076   0.0058
    equivalent-radius growth    1044        0.118   0.0139

      detections that day  fire-days   r (p90)  r (radius)
                     >= 8       1044     0.096       0.118
                    >= 20        792     0.117       0.148
                    >= 50        484     0.121       0.191
                   >= 100        275     0.159       0.192
                   >= 200         93     0.192       0.234

    It does improve, from r = 0.10 to r = 0.19, exactly as the direction
    channel does. So this dataset cannot separate 'the speed is not in the
    data' from 'the speed is in the data and measured even worse than the
    bearing'. What it does establish is the operational fact: at the best
    measurement available here the advance still explains under 6 % of the
    variance in wind, against 0.33 resultant length for the bearing.

    Binning on the p90 measure:

         advance (m/day)      n  wind p25   median      p75   p10-p90
         375 - 706          209      2.50     3.20     4.20       91 %
