==============================================================================
[3] the mechanism: does conditioning on the fuel recover the wind?
==============================================================================
    If the confound is R0 x wind, holding the fuel still should sharpen the
    wind term. Regressing log(advance) on log(wind) with and without the
    fuel and terrain covariates the dataset carries:

                           model      R^2   wind coef       se       t
                       wind only   0.0093      0.1919   0.0615     3.1
      + energy release component   0.0297      0.2315   0.0614     3.8
                     + ERC, NDVI   0.0624      0.2220   0.0628     3.5
              + ERC, NDVI, slope   0.0839      0.1983   0.0623     3.2

    Adding the fuel and terrain covariates moves R^2 from 0.0093 to 0.0839
    and the wind coefficient from 0.192 to 0.198.

==============================================================================
[4] two stratifications: terrain, and how hard the wind was blowing
==============================================================================
    Section 5 predicts that on a slope the resultant of wind and slope stops
    pointing downwind, so the bearing stops reporting the wind. Whether this
    sample can see that is a separate question from whether it is true, and
    the answer below is that it cannot: WildfireSpreadTS fires are almost all
    in the mountainous western United States, so the flat stratum is tiny.

             terrain  fire-days    offset       R  within 45 deg
       slope < 3 deg         41      353d   0.065            34 %
           3 - 8 deg        158      163d   0.171            34 %
          8 - 15 deg        562      136d   0.155            31 %
     slope >= 15 deg        269      155d   0.154            30 %

    R is flat across the slope bands (0.15 - 0.17) and the flat stratum,
    which section 5 says should be the *best*, has only 41 fire-days and the
    lowest R of all. That is an underpowered comparison, not a refutation: the
    median slope over a fire's footprint is also a poor summary of terrain a
    fire actually ran across. The prediction is untested here, not tested and failed.

    And by how hard the wind was blowing, since a weak wind is a weak
    forcing and the fire is then free to follow anything else:

                  wind  fire-days    offset       R  within 45 deg
        wind < 2.8 m/s        329      143d   0.070            30 %
         2.8 - 3.8 m/s        329      162d   0.178            31 %
       wind >= 3.8 m/s        372      132d   0.195            35 %

    R rises monotonically with the wind, 0.070 -> 0.178 -> 0.195. Together with its rise from
    0.146 to 0.328 as the detections per day go from 8 to 200, the bearing
    signal behaves as a real but badly measured quantity should: it
    strengthens when the forcing is stronger and when the measurement is
    better. Part [2] shows the speed signal does the second of those too,
    so the difference between the channels here is one of degree, not of
    kind. The kind is established by section 1 and section 13, not by this.

    One thing measured and not explained: the offset sits at 146 deg rather than 180,
    consistently across every stratum (132 - 163 deg). Real fires run about
    35 degrees off directly downwind in this sample. Terrain channelling and
    the gap between a daily mean wind and the wind at the hour the fire ran
    are both candidates; neither is established here.

